(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2609010200-the-invariants-belong-to-the-authority-not-to-each-apex")
  (:adr/title "Proposed: the auth invariants belong to the authority, measured once, and to each credentialed surface — not to each apex")
  (:adr/status "proposed")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-01")

  (:adr/context
  "PROPOSED, NOT SETTLED. This is where a day of measurement points, written so
   that it can be argued with. The refutations are listed in their own section
   and each of them is cheap to run; if one comes back the other way, this ADR
   is wrong and should be superseded rather than amended.

   ADR-2608311600 scores six apexes against five invariants each and reports
   36.67/100. Three attempts to close its findings ended with the apex being
   sound and the axis asking a question that apex does not have
   (adr-2608319900, adr-2608320000, adr-2609010100). One is an anecdote. Three
   is a shape.

   The shape: applying five invariants to each apex measures each apex as a
   miniature of the whole design — its own ceremony, its own issuance, its own
   credentialed plane. That is the second identity per product which
   ADR-2608039950 forbids by name. The instrument's form contradicts the design
   it measures, and the authn Worker's own configuration already says apexes are
   not peers: AUTHN_PRIMARY_APEX is kotoba.cloud, and the comment beside it says
   anonymous first registration exists only on Kotoba's identity plane while
   other RPs must first prove a linked Stable Principal.

   Two premises are doing the work below. Both are measured.

   MEASURED — a Biscuit is verified with no secret. `kotobase.biscuit-grant`
   says so itself: it must be able to decide without any secret, and that is the
   whole reason the credential is a Biscuit and not a shared string. The four
   kotobase query surfaces wired on 2026-08-31 hold the root as a var, not a
   secret. Attenuation is the holder's and happens offline: append a block, hand
   it to an agent, the issuer is never consulted and the agent cannot widen it
   back.

   MEASURED, after this ADR first got it wrong — that itonami.cloud exists to
   free humanity from labour. The first version of this section said the
   premise was the owner's and was not written down anywhere in the tree. That
   was a failure of search, not an absence: the product states it in the first
   person, in the most public place it has, at the top of its own landing page.

     cloud-itonami — the operating system for how work gets done
     Liberate all humanity from labor.

   The miss has a shape worth keeping. The search was for the Japanese word
   for labour across repository documents; the sentence is in English on a live
   surface. Reaching for the tree and not the thing the tree deploys is the
   same error as reading a checkout instead of origin/main, one layer out. So
   the argument below does not rest on an undocumented premise. It rests on the
   product's own first sentence.")

  (:adr/decision
  "SUPERSEDED IN ITS CENTRAL PROPOSAL, 2026-09-01, by work that landed while
   this was being written. Kept because the ordering below is still live and
   because the way it was superseded is worth reading.

   This ADR proposed re-partitioning the axes — authority, surface, consent.
   Another session went one layer deeper and fixed the root instead: state what
   the DESIGN asserts, independently of what a probe can see, and only then say
   how each claim is covered. That is `audit/invariants` in
   90-docs/auth_plane_integration, landed as `State the invariants
   independently of what a probe can see`. Every axis declares which invariant
   it serves; coverage sorts the result covered / unobservable-with-a-declared-
   reason / SILENT; a SILENT invariant fails the run with exit 2, and so does
   the mirror case of an axis serving no declared invariant.

   That is better than re-partitioning and for a reason this ADR half-saw. It
   diagnosed the instrument as contradicting the design; the fix is not a
   better partition of the instrument but making the design's claims the thing
   the instrument is checked against. Re-partitioning axes still leaves axes
   invented from what is easy to probe. Their version found a real hole on its
   first run — controllers-are-rp-scoped had no axis at all, while the probe had
   been recording the rpId it needed since its first version.

   What survives below: the telos ordering, the consent-surface observation
   (which is about WHICH surfaces a claim covers, not about how invariants are
   grouped), and the struck item 3. What does not: the three-category split.

   ── original decision, kept for the record ──

   Split the invariants by what they are properties OF. THREE categories, not
   two — the first version proposed two and listed as a refutation that a
   surface where a person is expected per request would need a third. That
   refutation fired within a day, from the product's own landing page, so the
   third is written in rather than left as a hypothetical.

     CONSENT surfaces — a person's presence is the FEATURE, not the cost.
     KEPT DELIBERATELY SMALL: owner direction, 2026-09-01, that the vision
     governs and autonomy is preferred by default. So a surface joins this
     category by being argued INTO it, never by defaulting into it. Two are
     argued in below — money moving, and one identity joined to another —
     because the product itself stops for both. An operator console is not
     one until somebody says why, and neither is a routine read.

     The direction was given softly, as a preference rather than a decree, and
     is recorded that way: it settles ties, it does not overrule a measurement.
     Where a person's presence is genuinely load-bearing, saying so is still
     the right answer:

   itonami.cloud shows one on its first screen. A bot has done the work and
   stopped: x402 payment, 24.00 USDC, no transfer has occurred, approval
   required. Beside it the page states the principle — bots keep consequential
   authority explicit. The identity panel names the other: Kotoba Stable
   Principal is not connected to Itonami on this browser, Itonami keeps its own
   first-party session, Passkey and cookie are never copied between services,
   Connect this Principal.

   So freeing people from labour does not mean removing every human act. It
   means bots do the work and a person decides what is consequential — money
   moving, and one identity being joined to another. A ceremony on a consent
   surface is not overhead to minimise; it is the thing being bought. The
   argument is therefore sharper than the first version rather than weaker:
   minimise ROUTINE presence, preserve CONSEQUENTIAL presence, and never
   confuse an invariant that serves the second for one that inflicts the first.

     AUTHORITY invariants — measured ONCE, against the shared authn:
       a ceremony is live; it is one implementation rather than a second;
       it mints the one capability format; first registration has one plane;
       other RPs link a Stable Principal rather than mint their own.

     SURFACE invariants — measured per CREDENTIALED SURFACE, not per apex:
       the plane reads a presented credential; a refusal names the credential
       class; an authentication failure carries 401 or 403.

     A surface that requires no credential is not in the denominator. Not
     exempt — absent. It has no question to answer.

   Two reasons, and the second is the load-bearing one.

   IT MATCHES THE DESIGN. `plane-reads-capability` is the one axis that survived
   contact with reality this week: it was a true defect on four kotobase
   surfaces and is fixed there, it is a false negative on murakumo, and at
   itonami it points at a plane that is public by design. The other four assume
   a person is present at that apex.

   IT MATCHES THE PURPOSE, if the purpose is the one stated. Measuring the
   authority once IS one enrolment per person; measuring surfaces many times IS
   many agents with nobody present. The uniform form pushes the other way: six
   apexes, six RPs, six ceremonies, a person signing in six times. An invariant
   that multiplies acts of human presence is pointed away from a goal of
   removing human labour.

   The same premise reorders the open work, and it comes out close to the
   inverse of the probe's headroom order. That inversion is the strongest
   evidence for the split.

     1  itonami's holder-to-membership binding. Not a blocker to tick off: it
        is the seam where one human act becomes an agent's standing authority,
        and under the 2026-09-01 direction the shape is now decided even though
        the implementation is not: ONE human act mints an attenuated capability
        the agent then spends unattended, bounded by expiry, budget and scope.
        Not a credential the person re-authorises per request.
        Partly named by the product already: Connect this Principal joins a
        Kotoba Stable Principal to an Itonami session. That is a PERSON's
        continuity though, and a Biscuit presents an AGENT's holder. The open
        half is whether an agent's holder is bound through the person who
        connected it or separately. Do not read the existing kotoba-link path
        as having answered this.
     2  itonami's delegation layer, CACAO to Biscuit. Its own docstring says
        the Drive attests rather than alice proving she chose to. A delegation
        the person did not sign is the platform granting on their behalf.
     3  [STRUCK 2026-09-01] the mrb_ presentation divergence was listed here
        as agent labour. Measured the next day, it is not. An agent does not
        carry one capability across surfaces: it holds one per surface and
        presents whatever it was handed, so it needs no knowledge of shape.
        Presenting one apex's token at another fails on audience and root key
        regardless — kotobase answers malformed-token to an mrb_ token — and
        that failure is the cross-apex boundary this ADR wants, not a cost.

        The divergence is also narrower than stated: authn's verify-inference
        says in its own docstring that both the mrb_ form and the raw body are
        accepted internally, so it lives only in edge gates, where the prefix
        earns its place by rejecting garbage without a round trip.

        The party that needed to know the shape was the PROBE, because it
        fabricates tokens instead of holding one. That is already recorded as
        an instrument finding in adr-2608319900. Conflating an instrument's
        difficulty with an agent's labour is how a work list grows an item
        nobody needed, and this ADR asserted it without checking.
     4  auth hostnames for apexes with no principals. Last: it adds enrolment
        surface without adding anyone who can be freed.

   The probe puts controller-live (0.25) first. Under the stated purpose it is
   last.")

  (:adr/consequences
  "WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS. Each is cheap, and any one of them ends it.

     If the intent is that all six apexes become products with their own RP and
     their own issuance, the uniform scoring is correct, three apexes really are
     at zero, and this ADR is simply wrong. One sentence from the owner settles
     it and no measurement can.

     If x402.nexus has or will have end-user principals rather than operators
     only, its controller-live finding is real and item 4 moves up.

     If aozora's atproto identities are meant to link to the Stable Principal,
     that is authority-plane work with a real gap behind it, not an absent
     question.

     [FIRED 2026-09-01] A surface where a person is expected per request was
     shown by the product itself, one day after this was written: the x402
     approval step and the Connect this Principal ceremony on itonami.cloud.
     The split is three categories now and the consent one is above. Still open
     is which surfaces belong in it — an approval step and an identity link are
     two; whether an operator console is a third is not settled here.

     If offline attenuation turns out not to be reachable in practice — if
     every path ends up calling home anyway — then the labour argument
     collapses, because introspection per request is a central premise wearing
     an OAuth hat and no amount of splitting invariants changes that.

   WHAT IT COSTS. Re-splitting moves the denominator. The score will rise with
   nothing built, and a later reader will misread that as progress exactly the
   way 36.67 invites being misread as one third built. Any re-scoring must carry
   the old number beside it and say plainly that what changed is the question.

   And the direction itself has a price. Authority that works while nobody
   watches means a leaked token is good until it expires. Short expiry, budget
   ceilings and a revocation list get MORE important, not less, precisely
   because the person is not there. The widening landed in grant cbfa277 —
   permits? honouring a wildcard it used to discard — is correct and is also a
   widening, which is why the cross-apex boundary was pinned in the same
   commit.")

  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2608311600-one-auth-design-across-six-apexes"
